# Template only -- placeholders, no real values.
#
# The deployment-specific HALF of the `--env-file`. The other half -- every non-secret deployment
# setting -- is source-controlled, one file per environment:
#
#   deploy/env/staging.public.env.example
#   deploy/env/production.public.env.example
#
# Assemble the real file from both:
#
#   cat deploy/env/staging.public.env.example deploy/env/build.env.example > deploy/env/build.env
#   # then fill in the two values below and AVENLYO_RELEASE
#
# This file used to carry its own copies of NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL, NEXT_PUBLIC_AVENLYO_API_URL,
# AVENLYO_WEB_HOST and AVENLYO_API_HOST. That made it a second, hand-maintained deployment profile
# that CI and the production template could drift from -- and did. The public profile above is now
# the only place those values are declared; this file declares only what genuinely cannot be
# committed.
#
# Usage, once assembled -- the SAME file for every command in the deploy:
#
#   docker compose --env-file deploy/env/build.env -f deploy/compose.yaml build web api
#   docker compose --env-file deploy/env/build.env -f deploy/compose.yaml run --rm --no-deps -T api \
#     node dist/scripts/ops-preflight.js
#   docker compose --env-file deploy/env/build.env -f deploy/compose.yaml up -d --no-build --wait

# The browser-facing Supabase values. These are NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables: Next.js inlines them into
# the client JavaScript bundle at `next build` time, so setting them as ordinary runtime environment
# variables (the way web.env and api.env work) would have no effect -- the value has to exist before
# the image is built, not when the container starts.
#
# They are public by nature (an anon key scoped by RLS, a base URL), but they are still
# deployment-specific, which is why they live here rather than in the committed public profile.
#
# The service-role key is NOT one of these and must never appear in this file: a build arg becomes
# part of the browser bundle.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
